# deconvolutionNN - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a modern Python package for neural network-based deconvolution with a web GUI. The project follows strict Python best practices and modern development standards.

## Code Style & Standards

### Python Version
- Use Python 3.11+ features
- Include type hints for all functions and methods
- Use f-strings for string formatting
- Prefer list/dict comprehensions over loops when appropriate

### Code Organization
- Follow the src/ layout structure
- Keep modules focused and single-purpose
- Use relative imports within the package
- Place all tests inside src/deconvolutionNN/tests/

### Naming Conventions
- Use snake_case for functions, variables, and modules
- Use PascalCase for classes
- Use UPPER_CASE for constants
- Use descriptive names that explain intent

### Documentation
- Include docstrings for all public functions and classes
- Use Google-style docstrings
- Include type hints in docstrings
- Document exceptions that may be raised

### Error Handling
- Use specific exception types
- Include meaningful error messages
- Avoid bare except clauses
- Use context managers for resource management

### Security
- Never use eval() unless absolutely necessary and properly sandboxed
- Validate all user inputs
- Use parameterized queries for database operations
- Sanitize file paths and URLs

## Package Structure
```
src/deconvolutionNN/
├── __init__.py          # Package initialization
├── core/               # Core deconvolution functionality
├── models/             # Neural network models
├── utils/              # Utility functions
├── web/                # Web GUI components
└── tests/              # Test modules
```

## Dependencies
- Core: numpy, scipy, matplotlib, torch, streamlit, plotly
- Development: pytest, black, isort, ruff, flake8, mypy, pre-commit
- Documentation: sphinx, sphinx-rtd-theme

## Testing
- Write unit tests for all functions
- Use pytest for testing framework
- Aim for >90% code coverage
- Use descriptive test names
- Mock external dependencies

## Code Quality Tools
- black: Code formatting (88 character line length)
- isort: Import sorting
- ruff: Fast Python linter
- flake8: Style guide enforcement
- mypy: Type checking
- pre-commit: Automated quality checks

## Web GUI Guidelines
- Use Streamlit for the web interface
- Create intuitive and responsive layouts
- Include proper error handling and user feedback
- Use Plotly for interactive visualizations
- Follow accessibility best practices

## Performance Considerations
- Use vectorized operations with numpy
- Leverage GPU acceleration with PyTorch when available
- Profile code for bottlenecks
- Use appropriate data structures
- Consider memory usage for large datasets

## Best Practices
- Write self-documenting code
- Keep functions small and focused
- Use dependency injection where appropriate
- Follow the principle of least surprise
- Write code for maintainability, not cleverness
- Use logging instead of print statements
- Handle edge cases explicitly

## Common Patterns
- Use dataclasses for data containers
- Implement context managers for resource management
- Use abstract base classes for interfaces
- Implement proper __repr__ and __str__ methods
- Use property decorators for computed attributes

## Avoid
- Global variables
- Mutable default arguments
- Deep nesting (>3 levels)
- Magic numbers (use constants)
- Hardcoded paths
- Unnecessary complexity
- Premature optimization

## When Adding New Features
1. Write tests first (TDD approach)
2. Update documentation
3. Add type hints
4. Follow existing code patterns
5. Update requirements if needed
6. Run all quality checks
7. Update CHANGELOG.md

## Git Workflow
- Use descriptive commit messages
- Create feature branches for new work
- Squash commits before merging
- Keep commits atomic and focused
- Use conventional commit format

## Environment Management
- Use conda for environment management
- Pin dependency versions in pyproject.toml
- Use separate environments for development and production
- Document environment setup in README.md
